@page rh="DEUTERONOMY · 13 OF 15 · WHAT WAS DONE WITH IT" m="2 KGS 22–23; JOSH–KGS (NOTH, 1943); MATT 4.1–11; MARK 12.29; WINTHROP, “A MODEL OF CHRISTIAN CHARITY” (1630); FUGITIVE SLAVE ACT (1850); TKC: DOM"
# The book that wrote the rest
Everything here is reception, what people did with Deuteronomy.
rec:
A scroll in a wall | A law book found during temple repairs, read to the king and answered with a national purge. Whatever the scroll was, the narrative makes a found document the trigger for a political revolution, the most useful plot mechanism in the Hebrew Bible.
The history it shaped | Martin Noth argued in 1943 that Joshua through Kings form a "Deuteronomistic History," grading every king by this book's standards, chapter 12 above all. What looks like national chronicle is closer to an audit against a statute.
In the gospels | Jesus answers all three temptations with Deuteronomy (8.3, 6.16, 6.13) and names the Shema the first commandment. It's the book he quotes most in argument.
Covenant as politics | John Winthrop closed "A Model of Christian Charity" (1630) by paraphrasing 30.15–20, "Therefore let us choose life," and Puritan covenant theology read New England through the book's terms. The conquest chapters were read as precedent by people arriving on other people's land.
And the other way | Abolitionists cited 23.15–16 against the Fugitive Slave Act of 1850, and 24.7 with Exodus 21.16 against kidnapping and the slave trade. The same volume served the conqueror and the freed in the same century.
prompt: Where a found document could overturn something in my manuscript | fill

@page rh="DEUTERONOMY · 14 OF 15 · DOMINION'S DEUTERONOMY" m="DEUT 17.14–20; DEUT 15; DEUT 31.9–13; DEUT 28; TKC: DOM · SANCTUM"
# The half the Empire teaches
POSSIBLE: Everything on this page is a TKC construction to test against the manuscript.
tabrl:
| What the Sanctum circulates | What it keeps in the closed catechism |
| One sanctuary, one calendar, one authorized place of offering | The third-year tithe that stays in the town, and the tithe turned to coin and eaten by the household itself |
| The curses of chapter 28, as the explanation for every plague and defeat | The same covenant makes the sovereign subject to the document, with four named prohibitions |
| Obedience taught as loyalty to the Sanctum's reading of the law | The law is read aloud to the whole assembly, women, children and foreigners included, on a fixed schedule |
| The conquest chapters, as precedent for cleansing a province | The runaway-slave clause, the seventh-year release and the ban on interest among kin |
| Instruction as the Sanctum's office, delivered in its buildings | Chapter 6 puts teaching in the house, in the parents' mouths, with no priest present |
box: The argument in one paragraph
INTERPRETATION: Dominion keeps Deuteronomy's enforcement and drops its constitution. The curses, the central sanctuary and the war commands stay in general circulation. The law of the king, the release, the household school and the public reading go into the closed catechism. The Empire isn't opposed by a rival scripture. It's opposed by the second half of its own.
/end
prompt: Who in my cast has read chapter 17, and what they did the day after | fill

